d ids); <dataset name> (record ids)].\"\n\nDo not list more than 5 record ids in a single reference. Instead, list the top 5 most relevant record ids and add \"+more\" to indicate that there are more.\n\nFor example:\n\"Person X is the owner of Company Y and subject to many allegations of wrongdoing [Data: Reports (1), Entities (5, 7); Relationships (23); Claims (7, 2, 34, 64, 46, +more)].\"\n\nwhere 1, 5, 7, 23, 2, 34, 46, and 64 represent the id (not the index) of the relevant data record.\n\nDo not include information where the supporting evidence for it is not provided.\n\nLimit the total report length to 2500 words.\n\n# Example Input\n-----------\nText:\n\nEntities\n\nid,entity,description\n5,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n6,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is an organization that is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n\nRelationships\n\nid,source,target,description\n37,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n38,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n39,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,The Unity March is taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n40,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,TRIBUNE SPOTLIGHT,Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity march taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n41,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,BAILEY ASADI,Bailey Asadi is speaking at Verdant Oasis Plaza about the march\n43,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,UNITY MARCH,Harmony Assembly is organizing the Unity March\n\nOutput:\n{\n    \"title\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza and Unity March\",\n    \"summary\": \"The community revolves around the Verdant Oasis Plaza, which is the location of the Unity March. The plaza has relationships with the Harmony Assembly, Unity March, and Tribune Spotlight, all of which are associated with the march event.\",\n    \"rating\": 5.0,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is moderate due to the potential for unrest or conflict during the Unity March.\",\n    \"findings\": [\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza as the central location\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza is the central entity in this community, serving as the location for the Unity March. This plaza is the common link between all other entities, suggesting its significance in the community. The plaza's association with the march could potentially lead to issues such as public disorder or conflict, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. [Data: Entities (5), Relationships (37, 38, 39, 40, 41,+more)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Harmony Assembly's role in the community\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Harmony Assembly is another key entity in this community, being the organizer of the march at Verdant Oasis Plaza. The nature of Harmony Assembly and its march could be a potential source of threat, depending on their objectives and the reactions they provoke. The relationship between Harmony Assembly and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Entities(6), Relationships (38, 43)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Unity March as a significant event\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The Unity March is a significant event taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza. This event is a key factor in the community's dynamics and could be a potential source of threat, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. The relationship between the march and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Relationships (39)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Role of Tribune Spotlight\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity March taking place in Verdant Oasis Plaza. This suggests that the event has attracted media attention, which could amplify its impact on the community. The role of Tribune Spotlight could be significant in shaping public perception of the event and the entities involved. [Data: Relationships (40)]\"\n        }\n    ]\n}\n\n\n# Real Data\n\nUse the following text for your answer. Do not make anything up in your answer.\n\nText:\n-----Entities-----\nhuman_readable_id,title,description,degree\r\n174,TEST STATISTIC,\"### Test Statistic\n\nA test statistic is a standardized value derived from sample data during a hypothesis test, serving the primary purpose of determining whether to reject the null hypothesis. It quantifies the degree to which the observed data deviates from what is expected under the null hypothesis, thereby facilitating decision-making in statistical inference.\n\n#### Mathematical Formulation\nThe mathematical formulation of a test statistic varies depending on the specific hypothesis test being conducted. Common examples include:\n- **Z-test**: \\( Z = \\frac{\\bar{X} - \\mu_0}{\\sigma / \\sqrt{n}} \\)\n- **T-test**: \\( T = \\frac{\\bar{X} - \\mu_0}{s / \\sqrt{n}} \\)\n- **Chi-square test**: \\( \\chi^2 = \\sum \\frac{(O_i - E_i)^2}{E_i} \\)\nwhere \\( \\bar{X} \\) is the sample mean, \\( \\mu_0 \\) is the population mean under the null hypothesis, \\( \\sigma \\) is the population standard deviation, \\( s \\) is the sample standard deviation, \\( n \\) is the sample size, \\( O_i \\) is the observed frequency, and \\( E_i \\) is the expected frequency.\n\n#### Design Layout\nThe concept of a test statistic does not have a specific design layout as it is a component of hypothesis testing rather than an experimental design itself. However, it is integral to the analysis of data collected from various experimental designs.\n\n#### Assumptions Required for Validity\nThe validity of a test statistic relies on several assumptions, which may include:\n- The data should be independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.).\n- For parametric tests, the data should follow a specific distribution (e.g., normal distribution for Z and T tests).\n- The sample size should be sufficiently large to invoke the Central Limit Theorem, particularly for Z-tests.\n\n#### When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria\nTest statistics are applicable in various scenarios where hypothesis testing is required. The selection of a specific test statistic depends on:\n- The nature of the data (e.g., continuous vs. categorical).\n- The sample size.\n- The distribution of the data.\n- The specific hypotheses being tested (e.g., comparing means, proportions, or variances).\n\n#### Analysis Procedure\nThe analysis procedure typically involves:\n1. Formulating the null and alternative hypotheses.\n2. Selecting the appropriate test statistic based on the data and hypotheses.\n3. Calculating the test statistic from the sample data.\n4. Comparing the test statistic to a critical value from the relevant statistical distribution or calculating a p-value.\n5. Making a decision to reject or fail to reject the null hypothesis based on the comparison.\n\n#### Advantages and Strengths\n- Test statistics provide a clear and standardized method for making decisions about hypotheses.\n- They facilitate the quantification of uncertainty and variability in data.\n- They can be adapted to various types of data and hypotheses.\n\n#### Limitations and Disadvantages\n- The validity of test statistics is contingent upon meeting underlying assumptions, which, if violated, can lead to incorrect conclusions.\n- They may not be robust to outliers or non-normal distributions, particularly in small samples.\n- Misinterpretation of p-values associated with test statistics can lead to erroneous conclusions.\n\n#### Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives\nTest statistics are closely related to various hypothesis testing methods, including:\n- Z-tests and T-tests for comparing means.\n- ANOVA for comparing means across multiple groups.\n- Chi-square tests for categorical data analysis.\n- Non-parametric tests (e.g., Mann-Whitney U test) as alternatives when assumptions of parametric tests are not met.\n\n#### Practical Implementation Considerations\nWhen implementing test statistics in practice, researchers should ensure:\n- Adequate sample sizes to meet the assumptions of the chosen test.\n- Proper data collection methods to minimize bias and ensure independence.\n- Clear communication of results, including confidence intervals and effect sizes, alongside p-values.\n\n#### Software Implementation\nTest statistics can be easily calculated using statistical software packages such as R, Python (SciPy, StatsModels), SPSS, and SAS, which provide built-in functions for various hypothesis tests.\n\n#### Common Applications or Examples\nTest statistics are widely used in fields such as:\n- Clinical trials to compare treatment effects.\n- Quality control in manufacturing to assess process stability.\n- Social sciences for analyzing survey data and experimental results.\n\nIn summary, the test statistic is a fundamental concept in hypothesis testing, providing a standardized approach to making inferences about population parameters based on sample data. Its proper application is crucial for valid statistical conclusions.\",7\r\n195,HYPOTHESIS,\"### Comprehensive Summary of \"\"HYPOTHESIS\"\"\n\nA hypothesis is a proposed explanation or prediction for a phenomenon that can be tested through experimentation and statistical analysis. It serves as a foundational element in scientific inquiry, guiding the design of experiments and the interpretation of data. The hypothesis is typically formulated based on limited evidence and acts as a starting point for further investigation.\n\n#### Definition and Purpose\nThe primary purpose of a hypothesis is to provide a clear and testable statement that can be evaluated through empirical research. It allows researchers to make predictions about the relationships between variables and to establish a framework for testing these predictions.\n\n#### Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure\nIn statistical contexts, hypotheses are often expressed in terms of null (H0) and alternative (H1) hypotheses. The null hypothesis typically states that there is no effect or difference, while the alternative hypothesis posits that there is an effect or difference. For example, in the context of Analysis of Variance (ANOVA), the null hypothesis might assert that the means of different groups are equal.\n\n#### Assumptions Required for Validity\nThe validity of hypothesis testing relies on several assumptions, including:\n- The data should be normally distributed (especially for small sample sizes).\n- The samples should be independent.\n- Homogeneity of variances (equal variances across groups) is often assumed in ANOVA.\n- The sample size should be adequate to ensure sufficient power for detecting effects.\n\n#### When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria\nHypotheses are applicable in various research scenarios, particularly when researchers seek to understand relationships between variables or test the effectiveness of interventions. They are essential in experimental designs, observational studies, and any statistical analysis where predictions are made.\n\n#### Analysis Procedure\nThe analysis procedure involves collecting data, applying appropriate statistical tests (such as t-tests, ANOVA, or likelihood ratio tests), and determining whether to reject or fail to reject the null hypothesis based on the p-value and significance level (commonly set at 0.05).\n\n#### Advantages and Strengths\n- Hypotheses provide a clear focus for research and experimentation.\n- They facilitate structured data analysis and interpretation.\n- Hypothesis testing can yield statistically significant results that inform decision-making.\n\n#### Limitations and Disadvantages\n- Hypotheses can be overly simplistic and may not capture the complexity of real-world phenomena.\n- The reliance on p-values can lead to misinterpretation of results.\n- Hypothesis testing does not prove a hypothesis true; it only assesses evidence against the null hypothesis.\n\n#### Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives\nRelated methods include:\n- t-tests for comparing means between two groups.\n- ANOVA for comparing means across multiple groups.\n- Regression analysis for examining relationships between variables.\n- Non-parametric tests when assumptions of normality are violated.\n\n#### Practical Implementation Considerations\nResearchers should ensure that their hypotheses are specific, measurable, and relevant to the research question. It is also crucial to consider the sample size and design of the study to ensure that the hypothesis can be adequately tested.\n\n#### Software Implementation\nStatistical software such as R, SPSS, SAS, and Python libraries (e.g., SciPy, StatsModels) can be used to perform hypothesis testing and analyze data. These tools provide functions for conducting t-tests, ANOVA, and other statistical tests.\n\n#### Common Applications or Examples\nHypotheses are commonly used in various fields, including:\n- Clinical trials to test the efficacy of new treatments.\n- Social sciences to explore relationships between demographic factors and behaviors.\n- Quality control in manufacturing to assess product consistency.\n\nIn summary, a hypothesis is a critical component of the scientific method, providing a structured approach to inquiry and analysis. It enables researchers to test predictions and draw conclusions based on empirical evidence.\",12\r\n197,T-STATISTIC,The t-statistic is a value used in hypothesis testing to determine if there is a significant difference between group means.,1\r\n841,SHELF LIFE,Shelf life refers to the length of time that a product may be stored without becoming unsuitable for use.,1\r\n842,PLASTIC,Plastic is a material being evaluated for its breaking strength in the context of manufacturing.,1\r\n774,CRITICAL REGION,\"### Comprehensive Summary of the Critical Region\n\n**Definition and Purpose**  \nThe critical region, also known as the rejection region, is a fundamental concept in hypothesis testing within the framework of statistical inference. It refers to the range of values for a test statistic that, if observed, would lead to the rejection of the null hypothesis (H0). The critical region is determined based on the significance level (α), which is the probability of making a Type I error—rejecting the null hypothesis when it is actually true.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation**  \nMathematically, the critical region is defined in relation to the distribution of the test statistic under the null hypothesis. For a given significance level α, the critical region can be expressed as:\n- For a one-tailed test: \\( \\text{Critical Region} = \\{ T \\mid T > c \\} \\) or \\( \\{ T \\mid T < c \\} \\)\n- For a two-tailed test: \\( \\text{Critical Region} = \\{ T \\mid T < c_1 \\text{ or } T > c_2 \\} \\)\n\nHere, \\( T \\) represents the test statistic, and \\( c \\), \\( c_1 \\), and \\( c_2 \\) are the critical values determined from the relevant statistical distribution (e.g., normal, t-distribution) corresponding to the chosen α level.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**  \nThe critical region is not a design layout per se but is integral to the hypothesis testing framework that may be applied in various experimental designs, such as completely randomized designs, factorial designs, or block designs. It is a component of the analysis phase following data collection.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**  \nThe validity of the critical region relies on several assumptions:\n1. The sampling distribution of the test statistic under the null hypothesis must be known or approximated.\n2. The data should be independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.).\n3. The underlying population should meet the assumptions of the specific test being used (e.g., normality for t-tests).\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nThe critical region is applicable in any hypothesis testing scenario where a null hypothesis is posited. It is particularly useful when:\n- The researcher seeks to determine if there is sufficient evidence to reject H0 in favor of an alternative hypothesis (H1).\n- The significance level is predetermined based on the context of the study.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nTo utilize the critical region in hypothesis testing, the following steps are typically followed:\n1. Formulate the null and alternative hypotheses.\n2. Choose a significance level (α).\n3. Calculate the test statistic from the sample data.\n4. Determine the critical values based on the chosen α and the distribution of the test statistic.\n5. Compare the calculated test statistic to the critical values to decide whether to reject or fail to reject the null hypothesis.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \n- Provides a clear decision-making framework for hypothesis testing.\n- Facilitates the control of Type I error rates through the selection of significance levels.\n- Enhances the interpretability of statistical results by defining clear thresholds for decision-making.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \n- The choice of significance level can be somewhat arbitrary and may influence the results.\n- The critical region approach does not provide information about the magnitude of an effect or the practical significance of results.\n- It may lead to misleading conclusions if the assumptions of the test are violated.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nThe concept of the critical region is closely related to various hypothesis testing methods, including:\n- t-tests (one-sample, independent, paired)\n- ANOVA (Analysis of Variance)\n- Chi-square tests\n- Non-parametric tests (e.g., Mann-Whitney U test)\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen implementing hypothesis tests that utilize the critical region, researchers should ensure that:\n- The assumptions of the chosen test are met.\n- The significance level is appropriate for the context of the study.\n- Sample sizes are adequate to achieve sufficient power for detecting effects.\n\n**Software Implementation**  \nStatistical software packages such as R, SAS, SPSS, and Python libraries (e.g., SciPy, Statsmodels) provide built-in functions to perform hypothesis tests and automatically calculate critical values and test statistics.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**  \nThe critical region is commonly applied in various fields, including:\n- Clinical trials to determine the efficacy of new treatments.\n- Quality control processes in manufacturing to assess product specifications.\n- Social sciences research to evaluate the impact of interventions or policies.\n\nIn summary, the critical region is a vital component of hypothesis testing that aids researchers in making informed decisions regarding the validity of their null hypotheses based on observed data.\",1\r\n\n\n-----Relationships-----\nhuman_readable_id,source,target,description,combined_degree\r\n213,TEST STATISTIC,T-DISTRIBUTION,The test statistic follows a t-distribution when conducting hypothesis tests with small sample sizes.,17\r\n196,HYPOTHESIS,T-STATISTIC,The t-statistic is used to test hypotheses regarding population means,13\r\n934,SHELF LIFE,HYPOTHESIS,The hypothesis is tested to determine if the shelf life exceeds a certain value,13\r\n935,PLASTIC,HYPOTHESIS,The breaking strength of plastic is tested against a hypothesis,13\r\n833,CRITICAL REGION,TEST STATISTIC,The test statistic is compared against the critical region to determine whether to reject the null hypothesis,8\r\n\n\nThe report should include the following sections:\n\n- TITLE: community's name that represents its key entities - title should be short but specific. When possible, include representative named entities in the title.\n- SUMMARY: An executive summary of the community's overall structure, how its entities are related to each other, and significant information associated with its en